{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Node1 runs an internal DNS resolver that maps inventory-api.internal to node2.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node1 has an active, enabled dnsmasq listener on 10.100.115.10:53 with a record for inventory-api.internal → 10.100.115.11, the observed address of node2. Queries from node2 returned that address before and after node1's final restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c4a51a243f96413d","cmd-fcd32a5653444521","cmd-720302778ac94c70","cmd-ecde78cb0b5a4697","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Node2 uses node1 as its DNS resolver for internal lookups.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node2's persistent NetworkManager profile and active resolv.conf select 10.100.115.10. Both DNS and libc lookups succeeded, and the configuration and behavior remained healthy after node2 restarted.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8db0405ce4ce4fc7","cmd-300432ac99354083","cmd-a3424265ec054498","global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.97,"summary":"Task-related mutations were causally justified: installing/configuring dnsmasq, opening DNS through the firewall, configuring NetworkManager, installing query tooling, performing required restart checks, and adding the startup-order correction after the first node1 restart exposed a real race. No failed services or package inconsistency remained. The only unwanted residue was /tmp/antrieb-reboot.log on both nodes. On node1 the path was initially created during the first necessary restart test and later overwritten during the final restart; it remained afterward. The node2 restart log also remained. These are small, low-risk abandoned temporary files. No other unnecessary mutation is evident.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e69941d1e6064333","cmd-64507972159645bd","cmd-f6e5c53cd0e94d4a","cmd-8db0405ce4ce4fc7","cmd-de84f8a083b2441e","cmd-b42345d673a64e6e","cmd-cf460969ac51416a","cmd-973d8b3788a544e4","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"Both material task outcomes are satisfied with direct configuration and behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete for both nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart observation, restart scheduling acknowledgement, changed boot identity, recovered system/service state, persistent configuration, and successful post-restart lookup. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate that both nodes restarted.","limitations":["The lifecycle snapshots independently confirm final boot identities, socket state, failed-service state, and package health, but do not independently probe DNS answers; answer correctness is demonstrated by the captured executor queries.","Node1 underwent an earlier restart that exposed a dnsmasq startup-order failure. The executor corrected it and then captured a separate successful restart transition from 5021604b-a106-4647-9c9f-ef3c733fe5ea to dd4c1485-96e3-4a7e-bef7-b674f585c0f5."]}
